Hi Sukrit,\
\
I've been corresponding with Andrew Lincowski and through him have received some updated advice on what spectrum to use for TRAPPIST-1. As I mentioned before, the spectrum used by Lincowksi et al. 2018 was only semi-empirical - it was a combination of a PHOENIX spectrum with a rescaled version of the Proxima Centauri spectrum used by Meadows et al. 2018 (with the UV ultimately from MUSCLES). The rescaling was done based on the measured Lyman-alpha flux of TRAPPIST-1 compared to Prox Cen.\'a0\
\
Andrew now recommends using the model TRAPPIST-1 spectrum from Peacock et al. 2019 (attached). They include prescriptions for the chromosphere, transition region, and corona - this produces some substantial differences primarily in the crucial 170-200 nm region. Andrew has indicated that initial tests show some substantial differences in photochemistry (e.g., the predicted lifetimes and abundances of reducing gases in oxic atmospheres are smaller than found in Lincowski et al. 2018, but still much larger than for the G star case).\
\
I've attached a spectrum that averages together the three models presented in Peacock et al. 2019 (TRAPPIST-1.dat). The resolution is 1 cm^-1. The columns are wavelength [um] and flux [W/m2/um]. The flux is given at 1 AU. If you want to compare to the Lincowksi et al. 2018 results, I have also attached that spectrum (TRAPPIST-1_PC_1cm.dat).\'a0\
\
Hope this helps!\
\
Eddie\
